  "query_preview": "..(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 340| AM EST WED NOV 25 2026\n\nThe extended period opens Thursday night with an upper-level closed\nlow positioned just west-southwest of the forecast area over western\nand central Kentucky, with the ECMWF and GFS in good agreement over 48\nhours out. 500 mb heights are modeled around 546 dam, landing within\nthe 5th to 10th percentile of climatological norms Thursday evening\nbefore dipping into the 1st to 5th percentile overnight around 06Z\nFriday. This closed low will progress northeast through Thursday\nnight, arriving in the Northeast U.S. by Friday morning.\n\nFor sensible weather across eastern Kentucky, temperatures will\nstart off in the low to mid 40s Thursday evening under rainy\nconditions, with breezy southwesterly winds gusting as high as 30\nmph. Overnight, temperatures will fall into the 30s, ranging from the\nlower 30s along and near the I-75 corridor to the mid and upper 30s\nfurther east. This cooling profile will result in rain mixing with\nsnow and changing over to all snow in some spots before dawn. Snow\nratios are expected to range from 8:1 to 10:1 for a brief window\novernight, but will be much lower outside of that window. Any model\nsuites applying a flat 10:1 ratio throughout the event risk\noverdoing snowfall totals, as forecast soundings and surface\ntemperatures support a wetter snow or rain-snow mix.\n\nPrecipitation chances gradually taper off from west to east through\nFriday as the system lifts into the Northeast. West-northwest winds\nwill increase behind the departing low through Friday afternoon,\nwith the 12Z LREF ensemble indicating 25 to 35 percent probabilities\nfor gusts as strong as 40 mph across far eastern Kentucky. Conditions\nwill remain chilly in the post-frontal air mass, with temperatures\nstruggling into the lower 40s Friday before dropping into the 20s \nand lower 30s Friday night.\n\nBrief upper-level height rises and surface high pressure build into\nthe region on Saturday, allowing temperatures to rebound into the\nlow to mid 50s under a mix of sun and clouds. However, the next\nsystem approaches quickly as an upstream shortwave ejects out of the\nRockies and digs across the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes through\nSunday. Southerly flow ahead of an approaching surface cold front\nwill advect milder air into eastern Kentucky on Sunday, pushing\nafternoon temperatures into the upper 50s to low 60s before rain \nchances return along and ahead of the frontal passage.\n\nHigh pressure builds back across the Ohio Valley behind the front\non Monday and settles over the central Appalachians by Tuesday,\nbringing a stretch of dry weather to start the new workweek. There\nare some model discrepancies Tuesday and Wednesday, which have led\nto the NBM introducing a low 15 to 20 percent PoP for isolated\nshowers. Another upper-level trough looks to bring rain chances\nback to the area sometime near midweek. Temperatures will moderate\nMonday through Wednesday, with readings warming from the low 50s \ninto the upper 50s and overnight lows in the 30s.",
